# kwik-term

A lightweight Neovim terminal plugin for quickly opening, toggling, and sending commands to a persistent terminal.

## Features

* Floating terminal
* Bottom split terminal
* Persistent terminal buffer
* Send arbitrary commands to the terminal
* Run the current file with a custom command
* Configurable terminal size and border
* Configurable terminal keymaps
* Simple Lua API
* User commands for quick access

## Installation

### lazy.nvim

Using a local development copy:

```lua
{
  dir = "~/projects/kwik-term",
  name = "kwik-term",
}
```

For a GitHub repository:

```lua
{
  "Milan6299/kwik-term",
  opts = {},
}
```

Since `kwik-term` provides a `setup()` function, lazy.nvim automatically passes `opts` to it.

### With configuration

```lua
{
  "Milan6299/kwik-term",
  opts = {
    height = 7,
    width = 0.9,
    height_ratio = 0.9,
    border = "single",
    start_in_insert = true,
    keymaps = {
      hide = "q",
      exit_terminal_mode = "<Esc><Esc>",
    },
  },
  keys = {
    {
      "<leader>tt",
      function()
        require("kwik-term").toggle({ float = true })
      end,
      mode = "n",
      desc = "KwikTerm Float",
    },
  },
}
```

## Usage

### User Commands

Open a floating terminal:

```vim
:KwikTermFloat
```

Open a bottom split terminal:

```vim
:KwikTermSplit
```

Send a command to the terminal:

```vim
:KwikTermSend git status
```

Any shell command can be passed to `KwikTermSend`.

Examples:

```vim
:KwikTermSend ls -la
:KwikTermSend git status
:KwikTermSend uv run main.py
:KwikTermSend npm run dev
```

## Lua API

You can also use `kwik-term` directly from Lua:

```lua
local kwik = require("kwik-term")

kwik.open()
kwik.open({ float = true })

kwik.toggle()
kwik.toggle({ float = true })

kwik.send("git status")

kwik.run_current_file("uv run")
```

## Configuration

```lua
require("kwik-term").setup({
  height = 7,
  width = 0.9,
  height_ratio = 0.9,
  border = "single",
  start_in_insert = true,
  keymaps = {
    hide = "q",
    exit_terminal_mode = "<Esc><Esc>",
  },
})
```

### Options

| Option                       |        Default | Description                           |
| ---------------------------- | -------------: | ------------------------------------- |
| `height`                     |            `7` | Height of the split terminal          |
| `width`                      |          `0.9` | Width ratio of the floating terminal  |
| `height_ratio`               |          `0.9` | Height ratio of the floating terminal |
| `border`                     |     `"single"` | Floating window border                |
| `start_in_insert`            |         `true` | Enter insert mode when opening        |
| `keymaps.hide`               |          `"q"` | Hide the terminal window              |
| `keymaps.exit_terminal_mode` | `"<Esc><Esc>"` | Exit terminal mode                    |

Set a keymap to `false` to disable it:

```lua
require("kwik-term").setup({
  keymaps = {
    hide = false,
    exit_terminal_mode = false,
  },
})
```

## Keymaps

`kwik-term` does not impose any global keymaps. Add your own keymaps using your preferred configuration.

```lua
local kwik = require("kwik-term")

vim.keymap.set("n", "<leader>tt", function()
  kwik.toggle()
end, { desc = "Toggle terminal" })

vim.keymap.set("n", "<leader>tf", function()
  kwik.toggle({ float = true })
end, { desc = "Toggle floating terminal" })

vim.keymap.set("n", "<leader>tr", function()
  kwik.run_current_file("uv run")
end, { desc = "Run current file" })
```

### lazy.nvim

You can also define plugin keymaps directly in your lazy.nvim specification:

```lua
{
  "Milan6299/kwik-term",
  opts = {
    keymaps = {
      hide = "q",
      exit_terminal_mode = "<Esc><Esc>",
    },
  },
  keys = {
    {
      "<leader>tt",
      function()
        require("kwik-term").toggle({ float = true })
      end,
      mode = "n",
      desc = "KwikTerm Float",
    },
  },
}
```

## Structure

```text
kwik-term/
├── lua/
│   └── kwik-term/
│       └── init.lua
└── plugin/
    └── kwik-term.lua
```

## Why?

Sometimes you just need a terminal without leaving your editor. Although Neovim comes with a built-in terminal, it does not provide a pre-configured floating window or a convenient default mapping for returning from terminal mode to normal mode.

`kwik-term` provides a small API for keeping a terminal around and sending commands to it without adding a large amount of configuration or terminal-management machinery.

It is mainly designed for quick lookups, scratch terminal sessions, and running file commands such as:

```text
uv run filename.py
python filename.py
npm run dev
```

For complex, long-running, or important tasks, it is recommended to use a separate terminal.

